WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.919 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  nostalgia is a powerful emotion games  like Goldeneye and Diddy Kong Racing  were staples in my youth and ate up more  time than I'd like to admit but  revisiting these iconic title shows them  for what they've become outmoded dated  clumsy relics of a lost era but that  doesn't mean old games are necessarily  worse than new when it comes to  long-running series like battlefield and  Call of Duty  people often decry the newer releases  and longed for the good old days as it  were and to be frank I'm guilty of this  myself the first to call of duty games  stick in my mind that some of the best  shooter campaigns I've ever played in  the early battlefield games provided my  most memorable multiplayer experiences  to date and despite the sad state of the  series in present day I still think  about those battlefield rounds of days  gone by the wide-open maps the intense  focus on teamwork the sandbox chaos of  each match as it unfolded into a unique  tapestry of narrative but what was it  about the early battlefield titles that  got lost along the way  what happened to that magic well PC  purists have long been claiming the  battlefield 2 was the pinnacle of the  series and I'm inclined to agree  so after Aaron Samson's recent piece  claiming bad company 2 was the best of  the franchise I felt compelled to delve  back into bf 2 and see if the dolphin  diving nade spamming madness was still  everything my rose-colored memory was  making it out to be and to be completely  and utterly frank this game is still fun  as hell there are so many things to love  about battlefield to the introduction of  commander mode the first appearance of  squads the feeling of being an  inconsequential soldier and a massive  battlefield the wealth of classes and  most importantly the maps oh the maps  strike a car Ken Master Sharkey  Peninsula the revivified Wake Island  2007 these massive dynamic killing  fields for what made battlefield 2 so  memorable for me you could go from  holding down a terrace to city block to  advancing up a wide-open pasture within  the same minute in each segmented piece  of these maps felt independently  excellent as a skirmish setting while  maintaining a balanced cohesion with the  larger flow of combat battles felt like  they had almost tangible tides that  could be turned with a sneaky flag cat  from the rear you could sense that an  enemy's defense had been broken as they  fell back and your squad mates surged  forward for a capture and beyond that  these maps were so open there weren't  three routes you could choose to take  from the spawn point for the action  there was just the battle ahead of you  and your own decision-making and skills  to guide you creative approaches that  push the limits were met with success  instead of stifled by boundaries  battlefield 2 is a game that rewarded  players with a unique sense of freedom  and therefore personal responsibility  while one man could shift the momentum  of an entire match only in rare moments  this served to encourage teamwork in the  cooperation of squads instead of the  classic approach of grabbing a  disposable aircraft on your own and  parachuting into the action beyond that  a squads class diversity was essential  without an anti-tank a single enemy and  APC could repel an onslaught without a  medic here expeditions into enemy  territory would prove short-lived  the squad's spawn also only allowed  players to spawn on their squad leader  forcing whomever that was to move  carefully and make tactical decisions  based on group success not personal  success this was a game that took  sacrifice to win this was a game where a  squad leader would throw down an attack  order and the entire squad would  coalesce to capture a point as one unit  you've been playing this week I can't  remember the last time I felt so much  cooperation amongst a group of strangers  playing a video game over the internet  however I can't say that bf2 is  everything my time addled memories make  it out to be the grenade spam is truly  life ruining it's depression inducing  it's hair pulling Lee awful it has only  matched by the amount of claim or death  that I was subjected to these two  explosive accessories single-handedly  paint this damn near perfect game and  account for more than half of my debts  it's not that I disapprove of any means  beyond bullets the artillery strikes are  either devastating or highly satisfying  depending on which side you end up on  but they never leave you feeling cheated  in addition to this the aircraft and  battlefield are also a bit strangely  balanced the Jets especially are almost  too agile and powerful but the upshot is  that they seem to be made of paper a  single well-placed anti-aircraft  installation can repel an aerial strike  with ease but it doesn't often feel like  Jets interact with the battle in a  meaningful way they just do their damage  and leave a trail of butthurt in our  wake lastly I have to make mention of  the hit detection and weapon feedback  in the game there were moments when I  wasn't even sure that I had successfully  killed somebody without checking the  text log and the smallest ping spike  could mean that easy kills were lost in  the cyclone of frustration and latency  the foggy draw distances of 2005 and  often muddled textures didn't help us  and on occasion I felt confused as to  where my bullets were naturally going  battlefield 2 is one of a small handful  of games that I personally consider a  masterpiece it was such an enormous leap  forward from the first two titles that  the difference is undeniable and only a  year after the release of battlefield  vietnam almost a decade later  battlefield 2 somehow holds up in spite  of its rampant explosive spamming and  dated visuals not only does it live on  in my mind is a great game but somehow  it actually lives up to all those warm  fuzzy memories  [Music]

